UID:e160deb5fbbd2c461f6ad43237ef4652@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:Topic: Happy town: Why scaling community is not like scaling a
  product\n\nWhat does it take to build a less lonely city? Can we scale hu
 man connection the same way we scale a tech startup?\n\nHappy Town began a
 s a grassroots experiment in creating spaces where strangers could become 
 friends. Through recurring walks\, shared meals and community gatherings\,
  it has grown into an evolving network hosts creating opportunities for mo
 re satisfying connections across Toronto.\n\nBut growing a community comes
  with distinct challenges not faced by companies or start ups.\n\nWhat hap
 pens when growth creates pressure on the very relationships we’re trying
  to nurture? What if the most sustainable way to scale community isn’t t
 o build a better product\, but to build a movement?\n\nDrawing on the expe
 rience of building Happy Town and its evolution into Less Lonely Winter\, 
 a collective effort to bring 25+ free community events per week to Toronto
  throughout winter\, this talk explores our approach to community building
 . One rooted in the idea that nobody owns community\; and the belief that 
 connection is grown\, not found.\n\n‎\n\nSpeaker: Adam Chen (in-person)\
 n\nAdam Chen works in mental health communications by day at HF Care\, and
  grows community by night with Happy Town. He is a storytelling and live j
 ournalism performer\, producer and director who dabbles in app development
 \, having co-created the private-blogging app “Smaller World." Website\n
 \n‎\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Prese
 ntation and Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode o
 f Conduct: https://civictech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the
  Civic Tech Toronto Slack: https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout 
 Us:\n\nOur weekly civic meetups bring together Torontonians (designers\, c
 oders\, urban planners\, government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\, stud
 ents\, communications strategists\, community organizers\, and more) who s
 hare an interest in making Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, sustaina
 ble\, and equitable through design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be part 
 of it!\n\nFor more info: https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nSuggest a Speaker
 :&#x20\;\n\nInterested in speaking at a future event\, or know someone who
 'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hear from you: https://link.civictech.
 ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nSuggest a Venue:&#x20\;\n\nHave a space that cou
 ld host up to 80 civic-minded folks for an evening? Consider recommending 
